    "title": "Stern Symbol of Opposition to the Shah",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTBy Nicholas Gage Special to The New York TimesDec. 11, 1978Credit...The New York Times ArchivesSee the article in its original context from December 11, 1978, Section A, Page 10Buy ReprintsNew York Times subscribers* enjoy full access to TimesMachine—view over 150 years of New York Times journalism, as it originally appeared.*Does not include Games-only or Cooking-only subscribers.About the ArchiveThis is a digitized version of an article from The Times’s print archive, before the start of online publication in 1996. To preserve these articles as they originally appeared, The Times does not alter, edit or update them.Occasionally the digitization process introduces transcription errors or other problems; we are continuing to work to improve these archived versions.TEHERAN, Iran, Dec. 10 — Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini leads no political party, commands no army, has no foreign allies and has been exiled from his country for 15 years, currently living more than 3,000 miles away on the outskirts of Paris.Man In the NewsBut today he probably. holds more power over the late of Iran than any other man, including his adversary, Shah Mohammed Riza Pahlevi, who is backed by a wellequipped army, vast wealth and international support.Just the publication last January of a newspaper article mildly critical of the Ayatollah was enough to start a revolt across this country that threatens the very existence of the monarchy, whose ??oots in Iran go back uninterrupted for wore than 2.500 years.For the last 11 months, shouting demonstrators in nearly every Iranian town and city have waved the portrait or the lionine white‐bearded, blackturbaned religious leader as a banner Of war, while ripping up the ever present state portrait of the Shah and feeding it to countless bonfires.A Symbol of Pent‐Up RageFrom exile, Mr. Khomeini has become the symbol of all the pent‐up rage against. the Shah's rule; with its authoritarianism, corruption and emphasis on modernization instead of the conservative Islamic tradition that the Ayatollah represents.“ If Khomeini didn't exist, we would have had to invent him,” said a profossor at Teheran University.The person who has sent the faithful Shiite Moslems of Iran unarmed into the streets against the Shah's forces is a stern‐faced old man of 78 who glares fiercely out at the world from under heavy dark eyebrows.As worshipers flocked to his garden in a Paris suburb, the Ayatollah has announced that his followers must “sacrilice your blood to protect Islam and overthrow the tyrant” during the Moslem mourning period of Moharram, which lasts until Dec.. 29. It is estimated that since these solemn holidays began on Dec. 2, as many as 72 Iranians have carried out his command and died for his cause.Ruhollah Khomeini was born Ruhollab Hendi in 1901 in the small city of Khumain, about 18 miles south of Te heran. When he was a young man his father, Mustafa, was killed by the mayor of the town in a personal clash. Ruhollah, one of three brothers, studied at religious schools at Marvi and Qum in Iran and Najaf in Iraq, all holy cities to the Shiites. One brother became a lawyer and the other a notary.Brother Arrested as a LeftistIn 1930 one of the brothers was ar rested for leftist activities and Ruhollah took a new last name, Khomeini.From an early age Mr. Khomeini gained a reputation as an independent minded scholar. He taught at religious schools, first in Isfahan and then in Qum, but his interpretive methods of teaching comtemplative signs aroused criticism from the older religious leaders, who did not encourage innovative thought among young scholars. They had Mr. Khomeini shifted to teaching Islamic jurisprudence, which allows less room for interpretation.Ayatollah Khomeini has had two wives. The first one gave him a son, Mustafa, born in 1933. With the second, the daughter of a rich landowner from Gilan Province, he had a second son, Ahmad, and three daughters. Mustafa Khomeini died of natural causes last year in Iraq. Mr. Khomeini is living with his second wife in Paris, his first having died some time ago.In 1944 Mr. Khomeini reportedly contributed to a book denouncing the monarchy, but he survived, the accusation and was put in charge of students at Qum where he gained great popularity among the young mullahs. The older ones felt uneasy about the fiery professor who became known as being tough, courageous and militant.Modernization Program ResentedMr. Khomeini's ascendency began in 1963 when Iran was torn by religious demonstrations led by members of the Moslem clergy who were outraged by the Shah's “white revolution.” This program of modernization angered the mullahs in part because of a land reform that confiscated most of the land held by the clergy, who made up the second largest landholders in the country.The highest title a mullah can hold is that of ayatollah, or “reflection of Allah.” There are approximately 1,200 ayatollahs in the Shiite world today. and of them six men hold the title of grand ayatollah. These titles are granted not by the religious hierarchy but by the acclamation of the people, who gravitate to the religious leader they admire most.Of the grand ayatollahs at the time of the religious riots in 1963, only Ayatollah Khomeini steadfastly demanded the ouster of the Shah and refused to compromise when more than 200 Moslems were killed in the streets. In retribution for his stand, the Shah had Mr. Khomeini exiled, first to Turkey, then to Iraq. The Iraqi Government expelled him two months ago and he went to France.During his 15 years of exile, Mr. Khomeini has not modified his adamant stand against the monarchy, while the other five grand ayatollahs within Iran have expressed their views in more guarded terms.“In 1963, the other religious leaders backed off, but Ayatollah Khomeini fought on and this won him the support of the mullahs and most of the people,” said Mehdi Barzegan, a supporter of Mr. Khomeini who heads the Islamicbased Iranian Liberation Movement. “When the regime exiled Khomeini, they made him a martyr and gave him the force to build the movement.”Admired by Younger RadicalsFrom exile, Mr. Khomeini never stopped denouncing the Shah, winning the admiration of the younger and more radical members of the opposition.The Shiite clergy are entirely dependent on the contributions of their followers for their livelihood and the support of their many schools and charitable organizations. Thus they are quick to sense the prevailing mood of the people and to express it in their mosques. But from exile, Ayatollah Khomeini was denoUncing the regime with a frankness that no clergyman inside Iran could risk.The Government tried to undermine his influence by planting an article in a leading Iranian newspaper that questioned his piety, suggested Communist connections and even hinted that his ancestry was not pure Iranian but part A grandfather of Mr. Khomeini went to India in the 1860's and reportedly held a British passport for a time, but he was an Iranian.The article turned out to be a colossal blunder. As a direct result, sporadic demonstrations broke out in some of the more conservative religious cities of Iran, resulting in numerous deaths. After each riot and the traditional 40day mourning period for the dead, new riots broke out in sympathy.Envoys Sent by the ShahBoth the Shah and his Western advisers made the mistake of underestimating the strength or the opposition, led by Mr. Khomeini. When it became clear that the monarchy was in danger, the Shah sent envoys to Mr. Khomeini in Paris, attempting to work out some kind of compromise. But by then the Ayatollah had seen the extent of his support within Iran, and he would accept no deals.Now as his followers sacrifice their blood in the effort to overthrow the monarchy, many members of the opposition who outwardly stand behind Mr. Khomeini are privately worrying about what may happen if the Ayatollah succeeds in ousting the Shah.“Khomeini is not harsh like the Shah, but he is just as convinced that he has all the answers,” said a lawyer active in the opposition. “I hope we don't climb out of a ditch only to fall into a well.”Mr. Khomeini has said that it is not his intention to rule Iran if he succeeds in driving out the Shah and that he has already chosen a political ,leader, whom he will not name, to lead the country.AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTThanks for reading The Times.Unlock your complimentary access.Activate now for unlimited articles courtesy of your school.No payment needed.",
